course: myths
lesson: 1
title: What really happens to things you swallow
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Jake leans in at break, dead serious: "Swallow your gum and it sits in your tummy for SEVEN YEARS. My cousin's doctor said so." Everyone gasps, and somebody quietly spits theirs into a tissue.
explain: Food myths are delicious: a melon growing in your belly, eight spiders a year crawling into your sleeping mouth, crusts that curl your hair, a Mentos and cola tummy that goes BOOM.
explain: The truth is a long tube: what you swallow goes down to a stomach full of strong acid, on through the gut, and whatever your body cannot use comes out the other end, usually within a day or two.
explain: So a seed has no soil or sunlight to grow, gum cannot stick for years, and gas from fizzy drinks escapes as a burp; your body is not a sealed bottle.
explain: When a friend clings to a myth, ask kindly "who measured that?" or "how would anyone see it?", and remember some myths are jokes or kind tricks to enjoy, while a few (like "swallowing your tongue") can lead to harm and are worth correcting.
fact: Swallowed chewing gum cannot be fully digested, but it moves through the gut and comes out in a day or two; only swallowing big lumps very often could cause a blockage.
fact: The "eight spiders a year" story has no evidence behind it; spiders avoid big, warm, breathing, vibrating animals like a sleeping person, and swallowing one is very rare.
fact: Seeds cannot grow in your stomach, which is dark, very acidic and has no soil; swallowed pips simply pass through.
fact: Eating crusts does not make hair curly; curliness comes mostly from your genes and the shape of the tiny pocket each hair grows from.
fact: There is no good evidence that cheese gives you nightmares (the idea got a push from Dickens's Scrooge blaming "a crumb of cheese" for a ghost); a big, heavy meal late at night can disturb sleep, whatever the food.
fact: Apple pips contain a substance that can release cyanide, but the amount is tiny and a whole swallowed pip usually passes out unbroken; harm would need a very large number chewed at once.
fact: Coffee does not stunt growth, but it is still not good for children because its caffeine can spoil sleep and make you jittery.
fact: Spinach is healthy, but it gives no sudden muscles; muscles grow over months with exercise, food and rest, and Popeye is a cartoon that exaggerates on purpose.
fact: The famous story that spinach's iron was overrated because of a misplaced decimal point is itself disputed and partly a myth.
fact: Mints dropped into an open bottle of cola make a real fizzy fountain, but in a body the gas just comes back up as burps; the stomach does not explode.
fact: You cannot swallow your tongue, because it is fixed to the floor of your mouth.
fact: If someone has a seizure (a fit, when the body shakes and they can't control it), you must never put anything in their mouth; keep them away from hard things, time it and call an adult or 999 or 911 for help.
fact: A scary number, a cousin's doctor nobody has met, or half a headline is not proof; ask who measured it and read the whole thing, and remember one example proves little, while a record kept over many days shows much more.
exercise: Jake says swallowed gum stays in your tummy for seven years. What really happens to it? | It passes through and comes out in a day or two | It sticks to your stomach for exactly seven years | It melts away completely in the stomach acid | It wraps around your heart and stays there
exercise: Why is the "eight spiders a year in your sleep" story not believable? | Spiders avoid big, warm, breathing, vibrating sleepers | Spiders only ever climb up walls and can never climb down | Spiders sleep at exactly the same time as people | Your blanket always stops every spider getting in
exercise: Why can't a watermelon grow from a pip in your belly? | The stomach is dark, acidic and has no soil | Watermelon pips are dead before you eat them | Melons only grow in hot countries, not in bellies | The pip would grow but be far too small to notice
exercise: What mostly decides whether your hair is curly? | Your genes and the shape of each hair's pocket | How many bread crusts you eat each week | Sleeping with wet hair on your pillow every single night | How much milk you drink as a baby
exercise: Mia says cheese after six gives you nightmares. What is closer to the truth? | No good evidence for cheese; a big late meal of anything can disturb sleep | Cheese is proven to cause nightmares in everybody | Only blue cheese gives nightmares, not other kinds | Cheese gives good dreams, so it helps you sleep
exercise: Rory says one swallowed apple pip will poison you. What is right? | The poison-making substance is real, but one pip is far too little | Every apple pip is deadly, even when you swallow it whole without chewing | Apple pips have no such substance in them at all | Only green apples have pips that can poison you
exercise: Why should children not drink coffee, if it does not stunt growth? | Its caffeine can spoil sleep and make you jittery | It makes your bones shrink a little bit every day | It turns your teeth brown after just a single cup | It stops your brain from growing for a whole year
exercise: How do muscles really grow? | Slowly, with exercise, food and rest over months | Suddenly, after one big tin of spinach | Only by drinking lots of fizzy energy drinks every morning | By sleeping much longer than everyone else
exercise: Where did the spinach-makes-you-strong idea become so famous? | Popeye cartoons | A law passed by the king | A film about superheroes in space | Doctors' adverts on the radio
exercise: What happens when someone eats a mint and drinks cola together? | The gas mostly comes back up as burps | Their stomach bursts open like a bottle | Their stomach freezes solid for an hour | Nothing at all happens, not even a burp
exercise: Leo says you can choke by swallowing your tongue. Why is that wrong? | The tongue is fixed to the floor of the mouth | The tongue is much too big to fit down the throat | You can only swallow your tongue when you are asleep | The tongue dissolves if it goes into the stomach
exercise: Someone near you is having a seizure. What must you never do? | Put anything in their mouth | Move hard things away from them | Call an adult or emergency help | Time how long it lasts
exercise: Where did the cheese-and-nightmares idea get one of its famous pushes? | Scrooge blaming "a crumb of cheese" for seeing a ghost | A royal doctor's warning to the queen | A cheese factory's spooky advert | A science experiment on mice in the 1990s
exercise: What is the best first question about Jake's "seven years" claim? | Who measured it, and how could anyone have seen it? | Which flavour of chewing gum lasts the longest in your belly? | How many years does a sweet stay inside you? | Can you speed it up by drinking more water?
exercise: Grandad says a melon will grow in your belly, with a twinkle in his eye. What is the wise response? | Laugh along; it is a joke and he knows it | Tell him off for spreading false science at the dinner table | Refuse to eat watermelon ever again | Ring the doctor to check if he is right
exercise: Auntie Pat says crusts make your hair curly. What is a polite, honest answer? | "I'll eat them because they're tasty, but my hair's staying straight!" | "That's a lie and you know it, Auntie Pat." | "Then I'm never eating crusts again, I hate curls." | "Can you prove it with a scientific paper first?"
test: "u swallowed ur gum?? ur gonna die in 7 years lol" What do you tell your mate? | It just passes through in a day or two, chill | He's right, it's dangerous and you need hospital | Only mint gum stays, fruit gum passes | Drink vinegar to dissolve it fast
test: Your big sister whispers at a sleepover that eight spiders a year crawl into your mouth. Which question shows the story is shaky? | Who has ever counted spiders going into sleepers? | How many legs does each of the eight spiders have? | Which month do the most spiders crawl in? | Do the spiders prefer open or closed mouths?
test: What would happen if you planted one watermelon seed in a pot of soil and another in a jar of vinegar? | The one in soil might sprout; the one in vinegar would not | Both would sprout quickly in a few days, because seeds are tough | Only the one in vinegar would sprout | Neither could ever sprout in any way
test: "Shut up, Rory, apple pips are totally harmless and there's nothing in them." Who is closer to the truth? | Neither: the substance is real, but the amount in a pip is tiny | The mate shouting at Rory: pips have nothing in them | Rory: one pip will poison you straight away | Both: pips are harmless and also deadly
test: Uncle Steve says, "Coffee stunts your growth, that's why I'm short!" Which is true? | Coffee doesn't stunt growth, but it isn't good for kids' sleep | He's right, coffee shrinks bones in children | Coffee makes children grow faster than milk | Coffee is the best drink for children before bed, as it warms them
test: In a cartoon, a sailor eats a tin of spinach and his arms swell up at once. What's wrong? | Spinach is healthy, but muscles take months of exercise to grow | Nothing, spinach really works that fast | It should be his legs, not his arms | It only works with fresh spinach, not the kind you get out of a tin
test: "bloody hell Zara, ur stomach's gonna blow up!!" she has a mint and a sip of cola. What will actually happen? | She'll probably just burp | Her stomach will explode like the bottle | She'll float up because of the gas | She'll freeze inside from the cold cola
test: A boy collapses shaking at the park and an older kid yells, "Grab his tongue or he'll swallow it!" What should happen? | Keep him away from hard things, put nothing in his mouth, get help | Hold his tongue firmly with your fingers | Push a stick between his teeth | Pour water into his mouth to wake him
test: Mia had a bad dream on a night she ate cheese. Does that prove cheese caused it? | No; one night proves nothing, a dream diary over weeks is better | Yes, one bad dream after cheese is enough proof for any scientist | Yes, because her mum said so | No, because dreams only come from fizzy drinks
test: Which myth here is most worth correcting firmly, not just laughing at? | That you should grab someone's tongue in a seizure | That eating crusts makes your hair go curly | That a whole melon will grow in your belly from a pip | That cheese gives you nightmares
test: "my nan says eat ur crusts or no pudding, they make hair curly" What's the best plan? | Eat them if you like; enjoy the trick, but hair won't change | Refuse the crusts and argue until pudding is cancelled | Eat twice as many to get extra curly hair | Hide them, because crusts are bad for hair
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the fate of chewing gum inadvertently swallowed? | It traverses the digestive tract and is excreted within a day or two | It is retained within the stomach for a period of seven years | It is wholly dissolved by the gastric acid within minutes | It adheres permanently to the wall of the stomach and remains there for life
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a seed ingested by a person cannot germinate within the stomach. | The stomach is dark, highly acidic and devoid of soil | Seeds are rendered lifeless upon contact with saliva | The stomach is excessively cold for germination | Seeds germinate solely during the hours of daylight
exam: It is given that a person is experiencing a seizure. Select the response that is consistent with established first-aid guidance. | Nothing whatsoever is to be placed within the person's mouth | The tongue of the person is to be grasped firmly and withheld with the fingers | A rigid object is to be inserted between the teeth | Water is to be poured into the mouth of the person
exam: Ascertain the consequence of the ingestion of a mint confection together with a carbonated cola beverage. | The liberated gas is expelled principally by eructation | The stomach is ruptured by the pressure of the gas so liberated | The stomach contents are rendered solid | No gas whatsoever is liberated within the body
